86198402 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198402 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198402 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198402 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186367736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198402 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198402 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198402 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186405090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198402 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186405097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198402 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186405104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198402 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186405118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198402 #edb/ref 1039 "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198402 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198402 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d4d69d3c-3bb7-5e99-b606-1bbd538fa1c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198486 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198486 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198486 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198486 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198486 #edb/ref 1045 "Calculating Flux Through Closed Surfaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198486 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186199294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198486 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198486 #edb/ref 1050 960.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198486 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "2c7e700e-97c2-5568-92c3-9812910bbf91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198618 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198618 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161830" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198618 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198618 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/fd/fd18335a2034613658c65186c4fc6e3e74067e730183d3e66fbacf8ab3601928.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,z,\\,-y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198618 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ca709c99-e06d-5a3f-baf0-9520bf4a14bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198618 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,R=-y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-2x)-(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-2y)+(-y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,z,-y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199294 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199294 #edb/ref 1061 44432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199294 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1bb12849-8513-5a29-9551-63f2f3815039" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199294 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199316 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199316 #edb/ref 1061 44618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199316 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "766d1837-ad6a-5940-9b62-d25b3ab7823b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199316 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199317 #edb/ref 1061 44619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199317 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "df7f59a9-5505-5955-a72e-ed3764163143" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199317 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199468 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that when we measure the flux of a vector field through a *closed* surface, we always calculate with respect to an outward-pointing unit normal vector.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\langle x, \\,0, \\, -y^2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the conical solid shown below. \n\n![](images/0b/0be83ceada68d0b56be4872f21652a7d18932ff059ea704576ca3cce892fa87b.png)\n\n \n\nLet's calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$ \n\nFirst, since $S$ is a closed surface, we have that\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z = g(x,y) = 2 - \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}$ is oriented *upward*, and\n\n- the lower surface $S_2: z = h(x,y) = 0$ is oriented *downward*.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- First, we consider the upper cone $S_1.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\nP = x, \\qquad\nQ = 0, \\qquad\nR = -y^2.\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-x\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-0\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+(-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Next, we consider the lower plane $S_2.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n} = \\langle 0,0,-1 \\rangle.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨x,0,-{y}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across the entire surface is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199468 #edb/ref 1065 7651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199468 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "73467c61-810e-5e81-abcc-b5eb99d1e0ab" #edb/ref 175921